13.2 What a web page is
A web page is a text file written in HTML (HyperText Markup Language). The browser reads the file and draws the page. The text is marked up with tags in angle brackets. Most tags come in pairs, an opening tag like &lt;p&gt; and a closing tag like &lt;/p&gt;, with the content in between. Together they make an element.
Here are the tags we meet most often.






Tag
What it holds




&lt;h1&gt;, &lt;h2&gt;

Headings


&lt;p&gt;
A paragraph of text


&lt;a href="..."&gt;
A link. The address it points to is in href




&lt;ul&gt;, &lt;li&gt;

A list, and an item of the list



&lt;table&gt;, &lt;tr&gt;, &lt;th&gt;, &lt;td&gt;

A table, a row, a header cell and a data cell



&lt;div&gt;, &lt;span&gt;

Containers used to group things, for layout



An opening tag may carry attributes, written as name="value". Two attributes matter most for scraping. The class attribute puts an element in a named group, such as class="amount", usually so that the page can style all amounts alike. The id attribute gives an element a name that is unique on the page, such as id="tender-list". Elements sit inside other elements, like boxes inside boxes. A &lt;td&gt; is inside a &lt;tr&gt;, which is inside a &lt;table&gt;.
Let us make a tiny page with minimal_html(), which turns a piece of HTML in a string into a page R can read.

tiny &lt;- minimal_html('
  &lt;h2&gt;Notice board&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p class="notice"&gt;Pre-bid meeting on &lt;b&gt;12-Nov-2024&lt;/b&gt; at 11:00&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p class="notice"&gt;Last date extended to &lt;b&gt;29-Nov-2024&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;a class="doc" href="nit-2024-131.pdf"&gt;Download NIT&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;table&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Item&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Rate&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Cement (bag)&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;420&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Steel (quintal)&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;6,150&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;')
tiny

{html_document}
&lt;html&gt;
[1] &lt;head&gt;\n&lt;meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=UTF-8 ...
[2] &lt;body&gt;\n&lt;h2&gt;Notice board&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;p class="notice"&gt;Pre-bid meeting on &lt;b&gt; ...


To get something out of a page, we take two steps. First we select the elements we want. Then we extract what we need from them. That may be their text, an attribute, or a whole table.

# all elements with class "notice", and their text
tiny |&gt; html_elements(".notice") |&gt; html_text2()

[1] "Pre-bid meeting on 12-Nov-2024 at 11:00"
[2] "Last date extended to 29-Nov-2024"      

# the address the link points to
tiny |&gt; html_element("a.doc") |&gt; html_attr("href")

[1] "nit-2024-131.pdf"

# the table, as a tibble
tiny |&gt; html_element("table") |&gt; html_table()

# A tibble: 2 × 2
  Item            Rate 
  &lt;chr&gt;           &lt;chr&gt;
1 Cement (bag)    420  
2 Steel (quintal) 6,150


That is the whole of web scraping in miniature. The rest of the chapter is about choosing the right elements on real pages, and cleaning what comes out.

13.9 Audit use case, payments to debarred firms
A debarred firm must not be given any work during the period of its debarment. Payments to such a firm, for work given during that period, show that the division did not check the list. Let us read the list from the portal. It is a plain table, so html_table() is enough.

debarred &lt;- read_html("Data/web/debarred-firms.html") |&gt;
  html_element("#debarred") |&gt;
  html_table() |&gt;
  transmute(firm = `Name of Firm`,
            from = dmy(`Debarred From`),
            to   = dmy(`Debarred Up To`),
            order_no = `Order No.`)
debarred

