  &lt;chr&gt;       &lt;date&gt;       &lt;chr&gt;             &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;chr&gt;   &lt;date&gt;     &lt;date&gt;    
1 PWD/AMR/040 2024-10-18   Vinayak Buildcon 547800 Vinaya… 2024-09-01 2027-08-31


In all, 3 payments worth ₹14,71,200 were made to firms during their debarment. Of these, 1 would have been missed by an exact match, even after cleaning.
What the auditor does next. For each of these payments, see when the work was given to the firm. A payment during debarment for work given before it may be in order under the rules, while work given during debarment is not. Find out whether the division checked the debarred list before awarding the work. And confirm that “Vinayak Buildcon” is the same firm as “Vinayaka Build Con”, from the PAN, GST number or bank account in the firm’s papers, before reporting it.

14.4 Splitting one data frame into files with many sheets
Sometimes we need to split the data into several files, and also each file into several sheets. For example, the pension data may have to be split into one file for each district, with a separate sheet for each scheme.
The library writexl writes a named list of data frames into one Excel file, one sheet for each. So we can write a small function which does the job.

book_and_sheets &lt;- function(df, x, y, folder){
  df_by_x &lt;- df |&gt;
    split(df[[x]])

  save_to_excel &lt;- function(a, b){
    a |&gt;
      split(a[[y]]) |&gt;
      writexl::write_xlsx(
        path = file.path(folder, paste0("data_by_", b, "_", x, ".xlsx"))
      )
  }

  iwalk(df_by_x, save_to_excel)
}

The function book_and_sheets() writes a data frame df into separate files based on the column x, and divides each file into sheets based on the column y. The files are saved in folder. Note that x and y are given as character strings, the names of the columns.
Let us split pension_data into files based on district, and sheets based on scheme.

book_and_sheets(pension_data, "district", "scheme", out_folder)
list.files(out_folder, pattern = "^data_by_.*\\.xlsx$")

[1] "data_by_Amarpur_district.xlsx"  "data_by_Belapur_district.xlsx" 
[3] "data_by_Chandpur_district.xlsx" "data_by_Devipur_district.xlsx" 

excel_sheets(file.path(out_folder, "data_by_Amarpur_district.xlsx"))

[1] "Old age" "Widow"  


Four Excel files have been created, one for each district, and each has a sheet for each scheme.

19.4 Audit tests on the full data
Now let us run some real tests, on all 24 lakh rows, exactly as we would on a small data frame.

19.4.1 Which DDOs rushed their spending into March?

march_rush &lt;- vouchers |&gt;
  summarise(total = sum(amount, na.rm = TRUE),
            march = sum(if_else(month(voucher_date) == 3, amount, 0), na.rm = TRUE),
            .by = ddo_code) |&gt;
  mutate(march_share = march / total) |&gt;
  slice_max(march_share, n = 10) |&gt;
  collect()

march_rush |&gt; mutate(across(c(total, march), \(x) round(x / 1e5, 1)),
                     march_share = round(100 * march_share, 1))

