We have kept the original text column untouched, and created a new clean_text column. This is a good habit. We can always go back to the original to read the full complaint.
Should we remove numbers? It depends on the question. Here we only want words. But if we wanted to know how much money was demanded, the numbers would be the most important part. We could extract them first with str_extract().

52.7 Problem 1, negation
Look at these comments, and the scores they got.

feedback_scored |&gt;
  filter(str_detect(comment, "\\bnot\\b")) |&gt;
  distinct(comment, score) |&gt;
  head(6)

# A tibble: 6 × 2
  comment                                                                  score
  &lt;chr&gt;                                                                    &lt;int&gt;
1 Food was not good.  Thank you, the operation was successful.                 3
2 Food was not good.  Treatment was done.                                      1
3 Toilets were not clean.  Treatment was done.                                 1
4 Staff was rude and careless.  Doctor was not available, very poor servi…    -2
5 Discharged after treatment.  Doctor was not available, very poor servic…     0
6 Toilets were not clean.  Very clean ward and good food.                      3


“Food was not good” got a positive score. The method saw the word good, found it in the dictionary as positive, and ignored the not before it. This is the biggest weakness of simple word counting. Let us see how often it happens. We use bigrams, from the last chapter, and look for a negative word followed by a dictionary word.

negations &lt;- c("not", "no", "never", "without")

feedback |&gt;
  unnest_tokens(bigram, comment, token = "ngrams", n = 2) |&gt;
  separate_wider_delim(bigram, delim = " ", names = c("word1", "word2")) |&gt;
  filter(word1 %in% negations) |&gt;
  inner_join(bing, by = c(word2 = "word")) |&gt;
  count(word1, word2, sentiment, sort = TRUE)

# A tibble: 6 × 4
  word1 word2     sentiment     n
  &lt;chr&gt; &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;chr&gt;     &lt;int&gt;
