2 FB0008      not      happy positive     -1
3 FB0010      not      happy positive     -1
4 FB0014      not      good  positive     -1
5 FB0020      not      clean positive     -1


Note that we added the previous column before joining with the dictionary. If we joined first, the words not in the dictionary, like not itself, would already have been dropped.
Now the score of a comment is simply the sum of these values.

score_comments &lt;- function(word_values) {
  feedback |&gt;
    left_join(word_values |&gt; summarise(score = sum(value), .by = feedback_id),
              by = "feedback_id") |&gt;
    mutate(score = replace_na(score, 0))
}

feedback_fixed &lt;- score_comments(feedback_words_fixed)

cor(feedback_fixed$score, feedback_fixed$rating)

[1] 0.7814317


We wrote a small function, score_comments(), because we will use the same steps again. Custom functions were covered in Section 3.1. The correlation with the star rating has improved to 0.78.

56.7 Joining by location
In ordinary joins, rows are matched by a common value, like a hospital ID. In a spatial join, rows are matched by location. st_join() finds, for each point, the polygon it lies in, and attaches that polygon’s columns.
Our hospital table already has a district column, entered when the hospital was empanelled. A spatial join lets us check whether each hospital really lies in the district recorded against it.

hosp_check &lt;- hosp_sf |&gt;
  st_join(districts |&gt; select(district_on_map = district))

hosp_check |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  count(match = district == district_on_map)

# A tibble: 1 × 2
  match     n
  &lt;lgl&gt; &lt;int&gt;
1 TRUE    120


st_drop_geometry() turns the sf object back into an ordinary data frame, which prints more neatly. Here every hospital lies in its recorded district. In real data, such a check often finds mismatches. Some are errors in entering the district. Others may be beneficiaries or units recorded in one district to claim a benefit available only there.

